## Raw OpenGL 3.3

No library but GLFW: one source for the desktop and the web, the differences marked with `#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__`.

### A1, a turning cube

<div class="shots one"><figure class="figure"><a class="demo" data-label="Live" href="../../decks/lhtml-site/html/01_address/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../../assets/img/demo-a1.jpg" alt="A cube with a color per face" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><code>?speed=0.2&amp;tilt=25</code>: a preset per slide, no control on screen.</figcaption></figure></div>

- **Shows:** the smallest program for a slide, about 200 lines: a cube that turns by itself.
- **Teaches:** the four differences between OpenGL 3.3 and WebGL 2 (headers, first line of the shaders,
  size of the canvas, main loop), and settings read from the address.
- **Trap:** `glfwCreateWindow(800, 600)` resizes the canvas, and the drawing takes a corner of the frame;
  create the window at the size of the canvas.
- [Sources and settings]({{ repo }}/tree/main/examples/a1-opengl-cube)

### A2, uniforms driven live

<div class="shots one"><figure class="figure"><a class="demo" data-label="Live" href="../../decks/lhtml-site/html/02_panel/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../../assets/img/demo-a2.jpg" alt="A box twisted around its vertical axis" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><code>controls=twist,hue,speed,light,reset</code>: the panel comes from the C++ declarations.</figcaption></figure></div>

- **Shows:** the box of A1, subdivided and twisted in the vertex shader, lit without normals.
- **Teaches:** parameters declared once (`slide::param`, `slide::action`), sent as uniforms at each frame;
  an angle accumulated, so that a change of speed does not make the box jump.
- **Trap:** the normal from the derivatives of the position (`dFdx`, `dFdy`) is flat per triangle: thin
  bands on the twisted faces.
- [Sources and settings]({{ repo }}/tree/main/examples/a2-opengl-uniforms)

## CGP

The examples hold only the files that differ from the template project of CGP; the build script of the
repository adds them to a copy of the template, with the bridge.

### C1, the CGP template, as is

<div class="shots one"><figure class="figure"><a class="demo" data-label="Live" href="../../demos/c1/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../../assets/img/demo-c1.jpg" alt="The scene of the CGP template: a sphere, a cylinder, the frame, and the ImGui window" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>The template of CGP, not one line changed.</figcaption></figure></div>

- **Shows:** that a CGP program works in a slide as it is: the orbit camera, the ImGui window.
- **Teaches:** what the page of the demo adds (frame filled, arrow keys sent to the deck, fade-in,
  readiness for the PDF).
- [Sources and settings]({{ repo }}/tree/main/examples/c1-cgp-template)

### C3, a waving surface

<div class="shots one"><figure class="figure"><a class="demo" data-label="Live" href="../../decks/lhtml-site/html/03_slide-buttons/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../../assets/img/demo-c3.jpg" alt="A ripple on a square surface" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Ripple, Waves, Drop: buttons in the text of the slide, and a panel in the demo.</figcaption></figure></div>

- **Shows:** a grid of 120 x 120 vertices moved at each frame, a ripple or parallel waves.
- **Teaches:** controls in the slide with `slide-controls.js` (`data-demo-set`, `data-demo-value`, the class
  `on`); one line changed in `main.cpp`; the ImGui window hidden in a slide.
- **Trap:** `draw_wireframe` uses `glPolygonMode`, which WebGL does not have: it draws nothing on the web.
  The wireframe here is drawn as segments.
- [Sources and settings]({{ repo }}/tree/main/examples/c3-cgp-waving-surface)

### C6, marching cubes with ImGui

<div class="shots one"><figure class="figure"><a class="demo" data-label="Live" href="../../decks/lhtml-site/html/04_imgui/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../../assets/img/demo-c6.jpg" alt="An implicit surface in the box of its domain, with the ImGui window" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><code>?gui_scale=1.4</code>: the ImGui window, readable in the room.</figcaption></figure></div>

- **Shows:** an implicit surface (blobs and Perlin noise) meshed by marching cubes at each change.
- **Teaches:** ImGui as the interface of a talk: enlarged from the address, its sections closed at start,
  the same variables also driven by the slide.
- **Trap:** two controls of the original example do nothing on the web (the wireframe, the export to a
  file): hidden with `#ifndef __EMSCRIPTEN__`.
- From the CGP example of [epita_ani3d_lab_code-2027](https://github.com/drohmer/epita_ani3d_lab_code-2027).
- [Sources and settings]({{ repo }}/tree/main/examples/c6-cgp-marching-cubes)

### C7, drag the points of a curve

<div class="shots one"><figure class="figure"><a class="demo" data-label="Live" href="../../decks/lhtml-site/html/05_drag/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../../assets/img/demo-c7.jpg" alt="A Bezier curve, its control points and its control polygon" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Drag a point: the slide shows the new length of the curve.</figcaption></figure></div>

- **Shows:** a Bezier curve or a Catmull-Rom spline, from 3 to 7 control points dragged with the mouse.
- **Teaches:** picking in 3D; a value computed by the program and shown by the slide (`slide::readout`,
  `slide::notify`).
- **Trap:** most browsers draw WebGL lines one pixel wide: thin on a projector. A tube reads better from
  the back of the room.
- [Sources and settings]({{ repo }}/tree/main/examples/c7-cgp-drag-points)

## Test them, and yours

Each example has a browser test, described in its `demo.json`: the demo starts, fills its frame, changes
when a parameter changes, and lets the arrow keys reach the slide. The same script captures its poster.
Copy an example and its `demo.json` to test your own program the same way
([step 4](../../01_tutorial/04_talk-day/index.html#test-it-automatically)).
